{
  "description": "Why the block-lifetime rule is a fix rather than only a restriction. A name born inside a control-form body belongs to THAT ENTRY of the block (spec/execution-model.md:371-379,603-605), so a deeper recursive call cannot clobber the value a shallower one is still holding: each level of `shrink` keeps its own `:next`, and the unwinding prints 0, then 3, then 6 — three distinct values from three distinct bindings of one name. A runtime that let `:next` leak to the root scope would give every level one shared binding, and the shallower level would print whatever the deeper one last wrote. Added at @testing's request during the review gate, which measured that `spec/examples/12-fractal.logo` renders a DIFFERENT picture under this slice with zero diagnostics on either side — 125 differing event payloads — for exactly this reason. The examples gate asserts only that an example runs clean, so it is structurally blind to that change; this fixture is what pins the corrected behaviour so a regression cannot pass unnoticed.",
